WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Tokens should have lifetimes that match their purpose and risk.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.560
A password reset token may need minutes.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.980
An access token may be short lived.

4
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:17.840
A refresh token or API key may last longer, but then it needs stronger storage, monitoring, rotation, and revocation.

5
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.540
A token that never expires and cannot be revoked turns a small leak into a long-running incident.

6
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:30.140
Short-lived tokens reduce exposure windows, but they create renewal and user-experience tradeoffs.

7
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.360
Long-lived tokens improve convenience, but they increase damage if copied.

8
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.300
Good token design makes this tradeoff explicit for each token type.

9
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:51.240
The design should also define logout behavior, account disablement, password changes, MFA reset, role changes, tenant changes, and suspected compromise decisions.

10
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.520
Rotation reduces risk when a token may have been copied or used outside expected context.

11
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.980
Refresh-token rotation can also help detect replay when an old refresh token is reused.

12
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:13.160
Revocation requires an explicit design, such as server-side state, a revocation list, token versioning, key rotation, or another validation mechanism.

13
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.620
Stateless tokens do not become revocable just because the team wants them to be.

14
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.860
Replay resistance depends on the system.

15
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.320
Some flows rely on TLS and short lifetimes.

16
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.900
Some use one-time tokens.

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.280
Some use nonce-based protocols, proof-of-possession designs, token binding approaches, or strict server-side status checks.

18
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.780
The important point is to choose deliberately.

19
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:47.240
Token issuance is the beginning of the lifecycle; expiration, rotation, revocation, and compromise response decide how much damage a leak can do.
